CVPR-2024-P0005,CVPR-2024-A000024,Hanchao Liu,shared,3,5,5,"Constraints in Character Animation trajectory velocity keyframe interaction Prev: Individual Tasks Trajectory control contact physics combinations Ours: Open-Set Motion Control Task: “walking” + left hand always “walking and turning around”+ touching head (radius= .1m) inside a square (- <x< , - <z< ) / Decomposed Constraints / Motion Programming . Distance between left hand and head = .1m Motion in-betweening manipulation total_err = for frame in motions: total_err += | distToPoint( frame[‘l_hand’], frame[‘head’]) – . | “walking” + holding a ball (diameter= .4m) “walking” + holding a heavy ball (diameter= .4m) . All the joints fall inside the square (- <x< , - <z< ) . Distance between both hands = . m . No collision between ball and chest . Distance between both hands = . m . No collision between ball and chest . Center of gravity falls within the foot region total_err = for frame in motions: for joint in frame: total_err += max(-joint.x- . , )+max(joint.x- . , )+ max(-joint.z- . , )+max(joint.z- . , ) total_err = for frame in motions: dist_err = | distToPoint(frame[‘l_hand’], frame[‘r_hand’]) – . | hand_mid_point = midPoint( frame[‘l_hand’], frame[‘r_hand’]) collision_err = max( . + chest_thickness distToPoint(hand_mid_point, frame[‘chest’]), ) total_err += dist_err + collision_err total_err = ; body_mass = ; ball_mass = for frame in motions: dist_err = | distToPoint(frame[‘l_hand’], frame[‘r_hand’]) – . | hand_mid_point = midPoint(frame[‘l_hand’], frame[‘r_hand’]) collision_err = max( . + chest_thickness – distToPoint(hand_mid_point, frame[‘chest’]), ) ball = Ball(center=hand_mid_point, diameter= . ) balance_err = outsideRegionError(projToGround( massCtrTotal(frame, ball, body_mass, ball_mass)), computeFootRegion(frame) ) total_err += dist_err + collision_err + balance_err no collision contacts Human-scene interaction keep balance … Figure . We introduce Programmable Motion Generation as a solution for open-set human motion control. Unlike previous works that treat a finite set of motion constraints as individual tasks, we attempt to solve vast and novel tasks in a unified framework. Through Programmable Motion Generation, an arbitrary controlled motion generation task is effectively solved by simply programming an error function rather than collecting training data and designing networks. The programming is also able to be implemented automatically.",-14,data/raw/cvpr/2024/first_pages/CVPR-2024-P0005.txt,https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2024/papers/Liu_Programmable_Motion_Generation_for_Open-Set_Motion_Control_Tasks_CVPR_2024_paper.pdf
