CVPR-2024-P1789,CVPR-2024-A010061,Chen Wei,shared,3,6,6,"Johns Hopkins University (i) Transferable Prompts for Text-to-Image Generation (ii) Multi-Modal Dialogue What is unusual Prompt about this image? It is unusual to see a Bard corgi riding a bicycle, “a photograph especially in a busy closeup dog city like New York… corgi wearing white hat corgi yellow glasses…” Stable Diffusion Midjourney Imagen De-Diffusion “a photograph closeup dog corgi wearing white hat corgi encoder yellow glasses atop on red handle bicycle street nyc corgi smiling wearing an orange fedora smile yellow sunglasses people car multiple screen buildings street…” decoder (text2img diffusion) (iii) Multi-Modal Few-Shot Learning with LLMs Prompt Q: What is the tabby “a photograph a cat wearing reddish red wool sweater with cat wearing? A: Red sweater. glasses…” “a photograph Q: What is the corgi closeup dog dog wearing? corgi wearing white hat corgi Yellow sunglasses. PaLM A: yellow glasses…” Figure . De-Diffusion is an autoencoder whose decoder is a pre-trained text-to-image diffusion model. It encodes an input image into a piece of information-rich text, which mixes comprehensive semantic concepts present in the image to be a “scrambled caption”. We group semantics by color for illustration. De-Diffusion text can act as a flexible interface between different modalities, for example, enabling diverse vision-language applications including: (i) providing transferable prompts for different text-to-image tools, (ii) enabling text-only chatbots, e.g., Bard [",-2,data/raw/cvpr/2024/first_pages/CVPR-2024-P1789.txt,https://openaccess.thecvf.com/content/CVPR2024/papers/Wei_De-Diffusion_Makes_Text_a_Strong_Cross-Modal_Interface_CVPR_2024_paper.pdf
